# Pilot design: measuring the real save rate

The data contain no randomised campaign, so the save rate behind every expected value is an assumption ({{ default_offer.save_rate | pct(0) }} for the standard offer). The literature shows why it must be measured, not assumed: risk is not responsiveness [[ascarza2018](references.md#ascarza2018)], and proactive contact can even raise churn [[ascarza2016](references.md#ascarza2016)]. This pilot turns the recommendation into a controlled experiment [[kohavi2020](references.md#kohavi2020)].

## 1. Hypothesis and decision

- **H1:** among customers selected by the recommended rule, receiving the offer lowers {{ p.outcome_window_days }}-day churn compared with not receiving it.
- **Decision metric:** measured save rate q = 1 - churn(offered) / churn(holdout).
- **Break-even of the pilot list:** the recommended list pays for itself if q is at least **{{ be | pct }}** (all offers' save rates scaled proportionally); q is the average save rate of the list's offer mix, assumed to be {{ p.assumed_save_rate | pct }}. Expressed as the standard offer's save rate, the recommended list breaks even at {{ recommended.break_even.break_even_default_save_rate | pct }} and the smaller base-budget list at {{ break_even.break_even_default_save_rate | pct }}.
- **Decision rule, agreed before the pilot starts:**

| Result after the wave | Decision |
|---|---|
| 95% interval of q entirely above 0 **and** measured q at least {{ be | pct }} | **Go:** scale to the recommended budget |
| Measured q at or below 0, or the whole 95% interval below {{ be | pct }} | **Stop:** redesign offers and targeting |
| Anything else | **Extend:** run one more wave and pool the data; at most {{ cfg.project.max_extend_waves }} extensions, then stop |

The significance test (is there any effect?) is what the sample size below is powered for; the break-even condition (is the effect large enough to pay?) protects the budget. Section 3 shows how often this rule says go, extend or stop for different true save rates.

## 2. Design

| Element | Choice |
|---|---|
| Population | Customers on the recommended list for the wave (positive expected value, within budget and contact cap), after consent and Do-Not-Call suppression |
| Randomisation unit | Customer; random assignment with a fixed seed, stratified by churn-risk decile and assigned offer |
| Reported subgroups | Fibre vs other internet, monthly vs longer contracts, seniors vs others: the list is concentrated in fibre month-to-month customers ({{ profile.by['InternetService|Fiber optic'].list_share | pct(0) }} and {{ profile.by['Contract|Month-to-month'].list_share | pct(0) }} of the list), and seniors are the group the model calibrates least well |
| Arms | Offered (each offer type is its own sub-arm) vs holdout ({{ p.holdout_share | pct(0) }}, not contacted) |
| Wave size | {{ p.list_size_per_wave | num }} customers, the recommended list scaled to the {{ p.base_population | num }}-customer base ({{ p.contacted_share | pct(0) }} of customers): {{ p.arm_size_per_wave | num }} offered, {{ p.arm_size_per_wave | num }} held out |
| Outcome window | {{ p.outcome_window_days }} days after first contact attempt |
| Primary outcome | Churn (disconnection or port-out) within the window |
| Secondary outcomes | Offer acceptance; net value per customer (realised margin - offer and contact cost); complaints, opt-outs, Do-Not-Call registrations |
| Guardrail metrics | Contacted churn must not exceed holdout churn; complaint and opt-out rates; sample-ratio check between arms |

