| True save rate | Churn offered | Customers per arm for 80% power | Waves of {{ p.arm_size_per_wave | num }} per arm | Go after one wave | Extend | Stop |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{% for r in pilot_power %}
| {{ r.assumed_save_rate | pct(1) }}{{ " (break-even)" if r.is_break_even else "" }} | {{ r.treated_churn | pct(1) }} | {{ r.customers_per_arm | num }} | {{ r.waves_needed }} | {{ r.p_go | pct(0) }} | {{ r.p_extend | pct(0) }} | {{ r.p_stop | pct(0) }} |
{% endfor %}

The last three columns simulate the decision rule 20,000 times per row with binomial churn in each arm (seed {{ cfg.seed }}).

- To detect a save rate equal to the break-even ({{ be | pct }}) needs about **{{ p.per_arm_break_even | num }} customers per arm**; one wave provides {{ p.arm_size_per_wave | num }}.
- With one wave, the smallest detectable save rate is about **{{ p.minimum_detectable_save_rate_one_wave | pct }}**.
- Detecting the assumed {{ p.assumed_save_rate | pct }} needs {{ p.required_per_arm_at_base | num }} per arm.
- If the true save rate equals the assumed {{ p.assumed_save_rate | pct }}, the rule says go after one wave {{ p.at_assumed.p_go | pct(0) }} of the time. At break-even the chance is about even ({{ p.at_break_even.p_go | pct(0) }}); then the expected gain from scaling is close to zero either way, so a wrong go costs little.
- Per-offer sub-arms are smaller; offer-level comparisons may need two to three waves, so the first decision is about the list rule as a whole.
{% set s15 = stress.by['sample|' ~ ('%g' % stress.targets[0])] %}
- **Real churn is lower than the sample's.** At {{ stress.targets[0] | pct(0) }} average churn the list churns less and about {{ s15.pilot_per_arm | num }} customers per arm are needed to detect the assumed save rate ([analysis report](../reports/analysis-report.md#outside-the-sample-lower-churn-and-vietnamese-prices)); size the real pilot from the operator's own churn rate.

## 4. Cost, value and timeline of the pilot

- **Cost:** offer and contact cost of the offered arm about **{{ p.wave_cost | usd }}** ({{ p.arm_size_per_wave | num }} x {{ p.cost_per_contact | usd(2) }} average cost per contact under the recommended mix). Worst case, if no one is saved, the whole amount is lost.
- **Expected value:** the offered half is itself a campaign with an expected net value of about **{{ p.wave_expected_net | usd }}** at the assumed save rates.
- **Price of learning:** the holdout costs nothing directly; it forgoes about {{ p.wave_expected_net | usd }} of expected value. Knowing the true assumptions before a full wave is worth about {{ monte_carlo.by.evpi.mean_scaled | usd }} per wave across the base (expected value of perfect information in the [Monte Carlo](../reports/analysis-report.md#7-how-robust-is-the-answer)), and that knowledge serves every later wave.
- **Timeline:** {% for r in cfg.project.roadmap[:3] %}{{ r.phase | lower }} {{ r.weeks }} weeks{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}: a decision about {{ p.weeks_to_decision }} weeks after approval ([business case](business-case.md#roadmap)).

## 5. Analysis plan

1. Check the sample ratio (offered vs holdout) and balance of risk deciles.
2. Estimate churn by arm and q with a 95% interval (bootstrap or delta method); report by offer and by risk decile.
3. Estimate realised net value per offered customer vs holdout.
4. Update `configs/default.yaml` with measured save rates (by offer) and re-run `python -m telco_retention run-all`; the dashboard and reports then reflect evidence instead of assumptions.
5. After several waves, use the randomised data to fit an uplift model and target by responsiveness rather than risk [[devriendt2021](references.md#devriendt2021), [lemmens2020](references.md#lemmens2020)].

## 6. Ethics and compliance

- Holding out customers from an offer is acceptable because it is a discretionary marketing benefit, not an essential service; the holdout is temporary and random.
- Only consented, non-suppressed customers are eligible; contact windows and frequency follow national rules, for a Vietnamese operator Decree 91/2020 (calls 08:00-17:00, at most one advertising call per 24 hours) [[decree91](references.md#decree91)].
- The pilot is covered by the data-protection impact assessment for the churn-scoring model [[pdpl2025](references.md#pdpl2025)].
